// ─── SPEC: BOOT-RECONCILE-2026-08-10 ──────────────────────────────────────────
// Task: task(BOOT-RECONCILE-2026-08-10) | Priority: alta | Impl: DS [S] — PHASED, gate per phase
// Author: Claude (Sonnet) 2026-08-10 | Origin: rmw3 — "diagnostico detallado del
// proceso y metodo del boot... colisiones, cargas duplicadas, errores logicos,
// mcps sin cargar" | Status: DESIGN READY — P1/P2 DS-executable now, P3 gated
//
// Scope note (compatibility): this spec targets what a DS/opencode session can
// actually reach — files inside ~/vsf and ~/state-canon-mcp. It explicitly
// EXCLUDES findings that live only inside ~/.claude/ (Claude Code's own global
// hooks, settings.local.json's MCP-approval cache, a `/context` self-report
// artifact) — those are [O]'s (Claude Code harness) to fix, not canon DS edits,
// and DS's own opencode session likely doesn't even see them fire. See §5.
// DS: if any of that assumption is wrong for your session, say so in your
// evaluation — that answer is itself part of what this spec asks for (§4).

target_files = "
  /home/rmw3/state-canon-mcp/instances/tasks_provider.py   (the bug, P2)
  /home/rmw3/vsf/CLAUDE.md                                  (the doc fix, P1)
  /home/rmw3/vsf/scripts/state/onboard.sh                   (reconciler hook, P2)
  /home/rmw3/vsf/TASKS.vsm                                  (this task's own entry)"

// ─── 1. CLAUDE.md's hook table is stale (mechanical, verified) ────────────────

fact("hook-table-drift") = {
  body: "CLAUDE.md's boot manifest documents:
    '.claude/hooks/vsf-onboard-reminder | SessionStart: compact, resume'
  The actual /home/rmw3/vsf/.claude/settings.json (read directly, not recalled)
  fires that hook on FOUR matchers: compact, resume, startup, clear. The 4th
  (startup) is the fix HANDOFF.vsm.md's 2026-08-10-claude-boot-audit block
  itself records making — CLAUDE.md's table was never updated in that same
  commit, violating CLAUDE.md's own stated rule ('if you add an artifact to
  the boot, add it here in the same commit').
  Fix: update the table row to list all four matchers, sourced from settings.json
  directly (cat the file, do not trust this spec's copy — it can go stale too).",
  why: "The single document whose job is 'never let the boot description drift
        from the boot mechanism' currently misdescribes its own most load-bearing
        hook. Cheapest, highest-confidence item in this spec — do it first.",
}

// ─── 2. state-canon MCP silently drops 2 real tasks (confirmed live, P2) ──────

fact("parser-divergence") = {
  body: "Two independent parsers read TASKS.vsm for the SAME purpose (open-task
    census) and currently disagree:
      - scripts/state/onboard.sh — inline Python, written 2026-08-10 boot audit,
        handles 3 known task() syntaxes + indented defs + duplicate-id detection.
      - state-canon MCP's tasks_provider.py — independent implementation,
        last touched 2026-07-30 (state-canon-mcp task/focus layer v0.8.1).
    Measured this session, same TASKS.vsm, same moment:
      onboard.sh:        351 task ids total · 30 open+partial
      state-canon MCP:   349 task ids total (meta.tasks_count) · 28 open+partial
                          (23 via state_query(tasks,{status:open})
                           + 5 via state_query(tasks,{status:partial}))
    Diffed the ID sets by hand. Exactly 2 tasks present in onboard.sh's output
    and COMPLETELY ABSENT from state-canon (not misclassified — state_query
    with filter={id:...} returns [] for both, same as a nonexistent id):
      DASHAI-S4-LAB-EVAL      TASKS.vsm:10061
      VSF-FEED-SPEC-REVIEW    TASKS.vsm:10078
    Both blocks carry the SAME literal comment on their status line:
      'status: open,   // declared 2026-08-10: had NO status field at all —
       open work invisible to every parser (DS census §2)'
    i.e. that status field was added specifically to fix cross-parser visibility
    for exactly this failure mode — and fixed onboard.sh, but NOT tasks_provider.py.
    349+2=351 and 28+2=30 — both discrepancies explained by these 2 blocks alone;
    no other divergence found in this pass.",
  why: "state-canon is the instrument CLAUDE.md's own manifest says to prefer.
        If it silently drops real open work, every session that trusts it over
        onboard.sh inherits a blind spot — the exact 'declared vs observed drift'
        class tasks_provider.py's own docstring says it exists to catch.",
}

fact("parser-fix") = {
  body: "1. Root-cause: read tasks_provider.py's block-recognition path
       (_BLOCK_START / _SINGLE_LINE_OLD / _parse_kv_continuation) against the
       TWO actual blocks at TASKS.vsm:10061 and :10078 — both use
       task(\"NAME\", priority=P2, agent=claude) = { ... } with an inline `//`
       comment on the first body line. Isolate what specifically drops them
       (a shared trait between the two — same header shape, same comment
       pattern, adjacent in the file — is the likely lead; do not assume,
       confirm against the actual regex/control flow).
    2. Fix tasks_provider.py so both blocks parse and return status=open.
    3. Do NOT special-case these two ids — the fix must generalize to any
       task() block sharing whatever trait caused the miss, or it is a patch,
       not a fix (R4: a fix that only passes its own two known instances is
       unverified against the class of the bug).",
  why: "Matches this project's own standing rule for this failure class —
        R4/R9: a divergence gets aligned or flagged, never silently patched
        around its symptom.",
}

fact("parser-reconciler") = {
  body: "Add a cheap cross-check so this class of divergence surfaces on its
    own next time, instead of requiring a manual session-side diff like this
    one. Two candidate locations, DS picks based on what's cheapest to wire:
      (a) onboard.sh §0: after computing its own total, query state-canon's
          `meta` domain (if reachable) and compare tasks_count. On mismatch,
          print the id-set diff (both directions), not just a count — reuse
          the exact caution onboard.sh's own §1 code comment already states
          for the rules-table check: 'Counting rows would report ✓ here...
          Equal cardinality is not agreement.' The same trap applies here:
          349 vs 351 is a visible mismatch, but two DIFFERENT wrong-by-2 sets
          that happened to net to the same delta would pass a count-only check.
      (b) tasks_provider.py itself: a self-test / reconciler class (it already
          has TaskSessionReconciler for a different drift class — the same
          pattern, a second reconciler) that asserts its own parsed count
          against a naive independent count (e.g. regexp count of top-level
          `task(` occurrences at column 0) and flags disagreement.
    Either is acceptable; onboard.sh's own doctrine ('every line here must
    change the agent's next action') argues for (a) — a boot-time flag beats
    a background one nobody reads.",
  why: "A one-time fix without a standing check just delays the next instance
        of the same failure to whenever someone happens to diff by hand again.",
}

// ─── 2b. Two opencode.json configs collide (found live, P0 — security-adjacent) ─

fact("opencode-config-duplication") = {
  body: "There are TWO opencode config files in this repo, not one:
      /home/rmw3/vsf/opencode.json           (root — declares mcp.state-canon-infra,
                                               fixed by commit 1a17b1f)
      /home/rmw3/vsf/.opencode/opencode.json (declares mcp.state-canon-mcp — the
                                               TASKS/focus/journal server, i.e. the
                                               thing fact(opencode-instrument-check)
                                               asked DS to confirm was missing —
                                               it was not missing, it was in the
                                               OTHER file)
    This resolves fact(opencode-instrument-check)'s open question: DS's
    'state-canon-mcp Connected' entry (visible in its live MCP panel) comes
    from .opencode/opencode.json, separate from the root file 1a17b1f touched.
    Neither config references the other; nothing declares which one is
    authoritative, or whether opencode merges both, or one silently shadows
    the other. That is itself the collision this whole spec exists to name.

    SEPARATELY, found while reading .opencode/opencode.json: it also declares
    a THIRD mcp entry, `stitch` (Google's remote design-tool MCP, enabled:
    false), with a literal `X-Goog-Api-Key` value inline in the JSON —
    committed to git since 2026-07-22 (commit 0bcb1fc), present in the
    CURRENT working tree and HEAD, tracked across 4 commits, origin is
    git@github.com:rm-w3kufe/vsf.git. Value NOT reproduced here (R9/security
    practice — do not multiply copies of a live-looking secret by quoting it
    in a second file). This is NOT DS's to fix: rotating a Google API key
    requires rmw3's action on Google's console, and removing it from the
    working tree does not undo the git-history exposure — that needs a
    separate, explicit, human decision (scrub vs accept-and-rotate). Held out
    of P1-P3 below entirely; see TASKS.vsm for tracking, gated on rmw3, NOT
    dispatched to DS as an executable step.",
  why: "A stray second config file is exactly the kind of place a credential
        goes unnoticed — this is not a hypothetical risk, it is what already
        happened here. Same failure class as the 2026-08-03 live NATS token
        commit this project already has an incident writeup for.",
}

// ─── 3. Instrument-scope question — DS drafts, rmw3 decides (P3, gated) ───────

fact("codebase-memory-scope") = {
  body: "A global (not project) hook fires on every VSF session start instructing
    'ALWAYS use codebase-memory-mcp tools FIRST for ANY code exploration...
    If not indexed, run index_repository FIRST.' Verified via list_projects:
    /home/rmw3/vsf is NOT indexed (only home-rmw3-bbh-wsl, home-rmw3-cv are).
    CLAUDE.md's own instrument table never mentions codebase-memory-mcp at all
    — it names only state-canon/state-canon-infra. Two live options, DS should
    draft the tradeoff for each, not pick:
      (A) index /home/rmw3/vsf and add codebase-memory-mcp to CLAUDE.md's
          instrument table with an explicit scope line ('code graph only —
          functions/calls/imports; never tasks/rules/services, those stay on
          state-canon'). Cost: an index to build and keep fresh against a
          repo that is mostly docs/spec/.vsm + scripts, not a typical
