  why: "Neither option is free, and CLAUDE.md's own philosophy ('anything here
        that starts to look like a source of truth is a bug') argues against
        silently doing either without saying so in the same commit.",
}

DECIDED 2026-08-10: rmw3 picked (B) with DS's kernel carve-out
(projects/vsf-nucleus/src, on-demand only). Implemented in CLAUDE.md's
Instruments section, commit pending this session. fact(codebase-memory-scope)
kept above for its reasoning trail — the decision itself lives in CLAUDE.md,
not restated here (doc-sync discipline: one home for status).

// ─── 2c. Indentation-anchoring divergence — verified, currently dormant ───────

fact("indentation-divergence") = {
  body: "DS flagged this during P2 as a SURVIVING divergence class, deliberately
    left out of P2's scope. Verified independently before dispatching this
    round: state-canon-mcp's three block-recognition regexes are genuinely
    column-0-anchored —
      instances/tasks_provider.py:53  _BLOCK_START   = re.compile(r'^(task|session)...')
      instances/tasks_provider.py:54  _SINGLE_LINE_OLD = re.compile(r'^(task|session)...')
      instances/tasks_provider.py:55  _HEADER_ONLY   = re.compile(r'^(task|session)...')
    none tolerate leading whitespace. onboard.sh's own parser explicitly does
    (scripts/state/onboard.sh:95, comment: 'form A/C: task(NAME): — leading
    whitespace TOLERATED on purpose' — a DELIBERATE choice, not an oversight).
    CURRENT STATE, verified live: grep '^\\s+task\\(' TASKS.vsm — 0 real
    definitions, only 3 prose mentions (task ids referenced inside sentences,
    not defined). checks/tasks-census-converge's own negative-control fixture
    already exercises exactly this class (that's WHY it was chosen as the
    fail-mode fixture — see fact in P2). So: dormant, not active. The risk is
    that the NEXT indented task() someone writes silently vanishes from
    state-canon the same way DASHAI/VSF-FEED did, with no warning on
    state-canon's side (onboard.sh DOES warn — see its 'INDENTED' check).",
  why: "Two parsers reading the same grammar should agree on what a valid
        definition IS, not just on how to classify one both already parse.
        onboard.sh's tolerance was a deliberate design choice — the
        asymmetry means tasks_provider is the outlier, not onboard.sh.",
}

fact("indentation-fix") = {
  body: "Recommend converging tasks_provider.py TOWARD onboard.sh's tolerance
    (onboard's choice is documented as deliberate; making tasks_provider
    strict was never a decision, it's just what column-0-anchored regex
    happened to do). Widen the three regexes to accept optional leading
    whitespace, same as onboard.sh's `[ \\t]*` prefix — mechanical, same
    class of fix as the brace-counting one, not a new design. Add a
    regression test with an indented task() block (mirroring
    checks/tasks-census-converge's existing fixture) so this doesn't
